WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.340
Printer cartridges sell for $25 while the die and glycol inside cost pennies to make, showing

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:12.040
how authentication chips and refill models keep prices far above the material floor. This

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:18.060
is First Principles Daily, Episode 86. A standard retail ink cartridge for a home printer

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:24.860
sells for roughly $25. Inside sits a few milliliters of die suspended in glycol along with

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.780
a molded plastic body and a small circuit board.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.740
The raw ingredients for the fluid itself can be synthesized for well under two cents. That

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:39.160
spread between material cost and shelf price is the opening for First Principles Examination.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:44.800
Printer manufacturers long ago shifted to a razor and blade model in which the hardware

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:50.140
is sold at low margin or even at a loss. The recurring revenue is expected to come from

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:55.380
replacement cartridges. Once that pattern became standard, every design choice reinforced

00:00:55.380 --> 00:01:00.640
it. The print head and nozzles are engineered with proprietary geometries and tolerances that

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.620
make third-party fluid incompatible. A small memory chip or authentication circuit is added so

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:12.400
the printer firmware can reject an unrecognized cartridge. Cartridge bodies are molded with unique

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.740
mounting features that prevent simple drop-in substitutes. Refill programs and subscription services further

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:24.380
lock the user into the original supply chain by offering convenience pricing that only applies to

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:30.600
genuine units. These features add incremental cost in silicon, molding tooling and firmware

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:37.100
development, yet they also create the barrier that sustains the $25 price. Retail channels and

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:42.260
packaging add another layer of margin that everyone in the chain treats as normal. The result

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:47.440
is an industry in which the physical object is treated less as a container of fluid and more

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.660
as a controlled consumable. No single company invented the full stack at once. Each element was

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:59.040
added over successive product generations to protect the revenue model. Customers accept the

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:04.440
price because the alternative appears to be either unreliable third-party ink or the hassle of

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:09.940
switching printers. One objection often raised is that the nozzles themselves require precision

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:15.640
manufacturing that must be paid for somehow. Yet the nozzles are only one small fraction of the

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:21.200
cartridge volume and mass, and their fabrication cost is amortized across millions of units once

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:27.020
the molds exist. The larger expense is not the physical nozzle plate, but the ongoing firmware

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.260
and legal work that keeps those nozzles from accepting fluid from any other source.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.620
Another common point is that at color consistency and archival quality demand expensive dyes.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:43.980
Commodity pigment and solvent prices show that the dyes themselves remain inexpensive at bulk scale,

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:50.340
so the consistency claim does not explain the full retail gap. The model therefore rests on the

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:56.420
installed printer base expecting a sealed, authenticated consumable rather than on any physical

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:02.520
limit of placing pigment on paper. If the cartridge could be assembled instantly from its constituent

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:08.960
atoms, the dominant expense would be the colorance and the carrier solvent. Commodity dye and glycol

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:14.400
together are reported in chemical industry data at a few cents per milliliter when purchased in bulk.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:20.680
A typical cartridge holds only a few milliliters, so the fluid alone sits comfortably below two

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:26.740
cents. The plastic body is a small injection molded part whose resin cost is measured in fractions

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:32.660
of a cent once the tooling is amortized. The authentication chip and flex circuit add perhaps

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:38.340
another few cents in silicon and assembly when produced at scale. Adding those elements together

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:43.520
produces a rough magic wand floor of five to ten cents for an entire finished cartridge.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:49.320
Against a twenty five dollar retail price, the idiot index therefore lands somewhere between

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:55.360
two hundred and five hundred. Walk through the arithmetic once. Twenty five dollars equals two

00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:01.280
thousand five hundred cents. Divide two thousand five hundred by five and the ratio reaches five

00:04:01.280 --> 00:04:07.120
hundred. That ratio is not a measurement of material scarcity. It is a measurement of how much of

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:12.680
the final price it carried by design decisions, authentication hardware, distribution agreements,

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:18.840
and the financing of the installed printer base. The largest single slice of the gap sits in the

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:24.620
authentication and nozzle proprietary systems because those elements require ongoing engineering,

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:31.120
firmware updates, and legal defense rather than raw feedstock. A secondary slice comes from the

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.400
retail and packaging chain that treats the cartridge as a high margin accessory rather than a

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:42.360
commodity fluid container. A third slice is the carrying cost of the razor and blade model itself.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:48.440
The printer is deliberately underpriced so every downstream component must repay that subsidy.

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:54.760
None of these layers is dictated by the physics of putting ink on paper. Each is an accumulated

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.780
choice that became self reinforcing once the industry settled on the current architecture.

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:05.640
An objection sometimes offered is that the retail price already includes research and development

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:11.120
for new ink formulations. Yet those development costs are spread across the same high margin

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:17.060
sales that the authentication system protects so the argument becomes circular. The price stays

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.760
high because the system prevents competition that would otherwise force those costs down.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:27.860
Another objection points to the environmental handling of spent cartridges. Collection and

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:33.780
recycling programs exist but they add only marginal per unit cost compared with the authentication

00:05:33.780 --> 00:05:40.460
and distribution layers already embedded in the $25 figure. The gap therefore remains overwhelmingly

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:45.920
a product of how the cartridge is allowed to function inside the printer ecosystem rather than

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:52.000
any irreducible material or regulatory burden. A redesign that started from the fluid and the

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:57.960
paper interface would first ask whether the nozzle array must remain integrated inside every cartridge

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:03.220
or whether a durable user replaceable head could be paired with a simple fluid pouch.

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:09.080
Such a separation would remove the need for proprietary nozzle geometry in every consumable.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:15.460
The second move would be to replace the authentication chip with an open mechanical or optical standard

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:21.380
that any supplier could meet without licensing fees. The third move would be to standardize the

00:06:21.380 --> 00:06:26.460
cartridge body across multiple printover ends so that high volume mold and runs could drive

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:32.220
the plastic cost even lower. Each of these steps would require either new industry standards or

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.960
regulatory pressure on interoperability both of which have proven difficult in consumer electronics.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:44.080
The hard part is not synthesizing the ink. The hard part is unwinding the installed base of

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:49.280
printers whose firmware expects the current cartridge format and the contractual relationships

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.900
that keep margins high. If those constraints were relaxed the idiot index could be driven down

00:06:54.900 --> 00:07:00.100
the by an order of magnitude simply by treating the cartridge as a fluid vessel rather than a

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:05.700
controlled revenue device. The prize would be visible first in reduced cost per printed page

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:11.320
for consumers and second in the emergence of third party suppliers who compete on fluid quality

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:17.540
rather than on circumventing chips. One practical objection is that a durable print head would still

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:24.000
need periodic cleaning or replacement shifting rather than eliminating cost yet a single durable head

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:29.720
could be engineered for far higher duty cycles than the throwaway heads now embedded in every cartridge.

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:34.680
Because the manufacturer would no longer need to tie head life to cartridge sales volume.

00:07:35.420 --> 00:07:40.080
A second objection concerns color matching across brands once bodies are standardized.

00:07:40.780 --> 00:07:46.240
Open fluid specifications could still allow each supplier to publish measured spectra and viscosity

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:52.260
data letting users or software choose the right pouch for a given printer without proprietary lock-in.

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:57.700
The remaining engineering task is therefore to define those interface parameters clearly

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:03.160
enough that multiple fluid vendors can meet them at commodity scale. When the dominant expense

00:08:03.160 --> 00:08:08.780
of a consumable lies in its lock-in hardware rather than its working fluid the opportunity is

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:14.540
to separate the durable interface from the replaceable contents. When an entire product category

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:20.180
prices itself against an installed base rather than against material physics the first company to

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:27.020
publish an open interface can reset expectations for everyone downstream. Tomorrow and every day after

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:33.180
one concrete example or one ripe opportunity. What would be the first public signal that a printer

00:08:33.180 --> 00:08:38.540
maker or an aftermarket supplier has begun to attack the cartridge idiot index at the nozzle

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:44.400
and authentication layer? That's a wrap on today's first principles breakdown. The ideas travel

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:49.240
further when you pass them on. Send this to a fellow builder. I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

